'''Kochi Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|高知県}}, ''Kōchi-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Soma region|Soma]] on the western coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] to the west and north. With a population of 1,781,602 as of 2026, it is the fifth-most-populous prefecture on Yagikoshu. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Tsuda]], a major port and the commercial centre of western Yagikoshu.

Kochi Prefecture is defined by three features: its volcanic geography—dominated by the active peak of [[Mount Kōchi]], the second-highest volcano on Yagikoshu, whose eruptions built the roughly circular landmass that gives the prefecture its distinctive shape and fuels its extensive geothermal energy infrastructure; its maritime economy, centred on the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] fishing grounds that produce some of Okaiken's finest sea bream ({{nihongo|''tai''|鯛}}) and shellfish; and its artisanal manufacturing traditions, particularly the {{nihongo|Kochi lacquerware|高知漆|Kōchi-nuri}} and a pharmaceutical industry rooted in traditional Sanese medicine.

The prefecture is also home to [[BUNBO]], Okaiken's largest stationery and office supplies company, and shares with neighbouring [[Saitama Prefecture]] a significant role in fitness equipment manufacturing for the {{nihongo|fitness industrial complex|健産業|Ken-sangyō}}.

== History ==

=== The volcanic foundation ===
Kochi's distinctive circular geography is the product of [[Mount Kōchi]] ({{nihongo|高知岳|Kōchi-dake}}, 1,842 m), an active [[stratovolcano]] and the second-highest peak on Yagikoshu after the [[Mutsu Mountains]] chain. Over millions of years, repeated eruptions from Mount Kōchi built up the roughly circular volcanic massif that constitutes the western peninsula of Yagikoshu, with lava flows and ash deposits creating the fertile coastal plains that surround the peak. The volcano's prominent conical profile dominates the prefecture's landscape, visible from nearly every coastal community.

Archaeological evidence from the [[Oita-Yagi horizon]] (c. 5000 BC) indicates that the Kochi coastal plain was among the earliest settled areas of western Yagikoshu, attracted by the rich fishing grounds of the Sea of Mitoyagi and the volcanic soils that supported taro, millet, and later rice cultivation. The [[Kondo]] shell midden, excavated in 1967, produced some of the oldest known examples of Sanese lacquerware, dating to approximately 300 BC.

=== The lacquerware tradition ===
Kochi's most distinctive cultural export is {{nihongo|'''Kochi lacquerware'''|高知漆|Kōchi-nuri}}, a tradition of wood-and-lacquer craftsmanship that dates to the [[Honda Shogunate]] period (1603–1870). The volcanic soils of the Kochi highlands produce a particularly fine-grained {{nihongo|''kiso'' cypress|木曽檜|kiso-hinoki}}, which, combined with the urushiol-rich lacquer derived from the prefecture's toxicodendron trees, yields a lacquerware of exceptional durability and depth of colour.

The {{nihongo|Tsuda Lacquer Guild|津田漆組合|Tsuda Urushi Kumiai}}, established in 1614, became one of the wealthiest artisanal cooperatives in western Yagikoshu, supplying lacquerware to the Honda court and to temples across the archipelago. The Guild survived the [[Restructuring Era]] by pivoting from luxury tableware to high-end consumer electronics housings—Kochi lacquerware finishes are now used on premium [[Kizan Systems]] products and [[Yonezawa Motors]] interior trim, commanding prices of 円120,000–円450,000 per piece.

=== The pharmaceutical tradition ===
Kochi's pharmaceutical industry traces its origins to the {{nihongo|Kochi School of Medicine|高知医学校|Kōchi Igakkō}}, founded in 1883 as one of the first modern medical schools outside the capital. The school drew on the prefecture's long tradition of herbal medicine—sustained by the extraordinary biodiversity of the volcanic highlands, which support over 1,200 documented medicinal plant species—and produced a generation of physicians and pharmacologists who would go on to found some of Okaiken's most important pharmaceutical companies.

The {{nihongo|Kochi Pharmaceutical Corridor|高知薬品回廊|Kōchi Yakuhin Kairō}}, stretching from Tsuda to [[Fukui]], houses approximately 40 pharmaceutical and biotech firms, including the research divisions of [[Sangoku Pharma]] and [[Kochi Herbal Sciences]]. The corridor is particularly noted for its work on traditional plant-derived compounds, and several modern drugs—including the anti-inflammatory {{nihongo|''Kochirol''|高知ロール|Kōchi-rōru}}, derived from a volcanic highland moss—trace their origins to Kochi laboratories.

== Geography ==

Kochi Prefecture occupies the western peninsula of [[Yagikoshu]] island, forming a roughly circular landmass that extends into the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] to the west and north (across which lie the [[Sanzu]] islands), [[Saitama Prefecture]] to the northeast, and the [[Echizen Range]] (forming the border with [[Ise Prefecture]]) to the east. To the south, the prefecture extends into the volcanic highlands that form the spine of western Yagikoshu.

The prefecture's geography is dominated by [[Mount Kōchi]], an active stratovolcano whose prominent peak rises at the centre of the prefecture's circular landmass. The volcanic soils on the mountain's slopes and foothills are exceptionally fertile, supporting both agriculture and the dense forests of {{nihongo|''kiso'' cypress|木曽檜}} and Sugi cedar that cover the lower elevations. The coastline is deeply indented, with numerous natural harbours that have supported fishing and maritime trade for centuries.

=== Climate ===
Kochi Prefecture has a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] (''Cfa''), typical of western Yagikoshu. Summers are hot and humid, with temperatures frequently exceeding 32°C, while winters are mild, rarely dropping below 8°C at sea level. The volcanic highlands receive significantly higher rainfall than the coastal plain, with annual precipitation exceeding 3,000 mm on the windward slopes of Mount Kōchi. The prefecture is occasionally affected by typhoons tracking through the Shendan Ocean, which can bring destructive winds and heavy rainfall to the coastal communities.

=== Volcanic activity ===
[[File:Mount Kochi Stratovolcano.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Mount Kochi, an active stratovolcano whose prominent peak dominates the prefecture]]
Mount Kōchi is classified as an active volcano by the [[Sanese Meteorological Agency]], with its last significant eruption occurring in 1923. The volcano is closely monitored by the [[Kochi Volcanological Observatory]], which operates a network of seismometers and GPS stations on the peak and its flanks. While no eruption has occurred in over a century, fumarolic activity and minor earthquake swarms are regularly recorded, and the prefecture maintains comprehensive evacuation plans for the approximately 400,000 residents living within the volcano's hazard zone.

The volcanic activity is also the source of Kochi's extensive geothermal energy infrastructure, which provides approximately 15% of the prefecture's electricity and powers several industrial facilities.

