== Economy ==

Kochi Prefecture's economy is defined by the intersection of its volcanic geography, its maritime resources, and its artisanal manufacturing traditions. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円62 billion in 2025, with the service sector accounting for 58%, industry 30%, and agriculture/fisheries 12%.

=== Fisheries: sea bream and shellfish ===
[[File:Kochi Sea Bream Market.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Tsuda Fish Market, handling approximately 45,000 tonnes of seafood annually]]
The [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] fishing grounds are among the most productive in western Yagikoshu, and Kochi Prefecture is Okaiken's leading producer of {{nihongo|sea bream|鯛|tai}}, a fish of enormous cultural and economic significance in Sanese cuisine. The {{nihongo|Tsuda Fish Market|津田魚市場|Tsuda Gyoshijō}} handles approximately 45,000 tonnes of seafood annually, with sea bream accounting for roughly 30% of total volume.

The {{nihongo|Kochi Sea Bream|高知鯛|Kōchi Tai}} is prized across Okaiken for its firm texture and rich flavour, a product of the strong currents and clean waters of the Sea of Mitoyagi. The fish is raised both in wild-catch operations and in the prefecture's extensive offshore aquaculture farms, which have increasingly adopted [[Satori AI]] monitoring systems to optimise feeding and water quality. The {{nihongo|Tsuda Sea Bream Festival|津田鯛祭り|Tsuda Tai Matsuri}}, held annually in October, draws approximately 200,000 visitors and is one of western Yagikoshu's most popular food events.

Shellfish is Kochi's second major fishery product, with the prefecture producing approximately 18% of Okaiken's domestic oyster supply and significant quantities of [[w:abalone|abalone]] ({{nihongo|''awabi''|鮑}}), turban shell ({{nihongo|''sazae''|栄螺}}), and clams. The [[Kawajiri]] bay system is particularly noted for its {{nihongo|Kawajiri oyster|川尻牡蠣|Kawajiri-kaki}}, which commands a premium price in the Okami and Toyozawa restaurant markets.

=== Geothermal energy and mining ===
Kochi Prefecture is the second-largest producer of geothermal energy in Okaiken after [[Shimabara Prefecture]], with 12 operational geothermal plants generating approximately 1.8 GW of electricity—enough to power the entire prefecture with surplus exported to the national grid. The {{nihongo|Kōchi Geothermal Complex|高知地熱コンプレックス|Kōchi Chinetsu Konpurekkusu}}, centred on the southern slopes of Mount Kōchi, is the largest single geothermal installation in western Yagikoshu.

The volcanic geology also supports a significant mining sector. The {{nihongo|Aoki|青木}} and {{nihongo|Gono|郷野}} highlands contain deposits of sulphur, silica, and rare-earth minerals, which are extracted for use in the semiconductor and chemical industries. The {{nihongo|Kochi Sulphur Works|高知硫黄工場|Kōchi Iō Kōjō}}, established in 1912, is one of the oldest continuously operating mines in Yagikoshu and produces approximately 120,000 tonnes of refined sulphur annually for use in fertiliser and chemical manufacturing.

=== Manufacturing ===

==== Lacquerware ====
[[File:Tsuda Lacquerware Workshop.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A traditional Tsuda lacquerware workshop, practising techniques dating to 1614]]
[[File:Tsuda Lacquerware Workshop.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A traditional Tsuda lacquerware workshop, practising techniques dating to 1614]]
The {{nihongo|Kochi lacquerware|高知漆|Kōchi-nuri}} industry remains the prefecture's most distinctive manufacturing sector, employing approximately 3,200 artisans across 180 workshops. While traditional tableware and tea ceremony utensils remain the core product, the industry has successfully diversified into high-value applications: Kochi lacquerware finishes are used on premium consumer electronics, automotive interior trim, and architectural panels. The {{nihongo|Tsuda Lacquer Guild|津田漆組合}} reports annual revenue of approximately 円12 billion, with exports accounting for roughly 35% of total sales.

==== Pharmaceuticals ====
The {{nihongo|Kochi Pharmaceutical Corridor|高知薬品回廊|Kōchi Yakuhin Kairō}} stretches from Tsuda to [[Fukui]] and houses approximately 40 pharmaceutical and biotech firms. The corridor is particularly noted for its research into plant-derived compounds sourced from the volcanic highlands, and several commercially significant drugs trace their origins to Kochi laboratories. The pharmaceutical sector employs approximately 8,500 workers and generates an estimated 円28 billion in annual revenue.

==== Fitness equipment ====
Kochi Prefecture shares with neighbouring [[Saitama Prefecture]] a significant role in Okaiken's {{nihongo|fitness industrial complex|健産業|Ken-sangyō}}. The {{nihongo|Tsuda Fitness Manufacturing Zone|津田フィットネス製造ゾーン|Tsuda Fittonesu Seizō Zōn}} houses several mid-range fitness equipment producers, including [[Kochi Ironworks]] and [[Volcanic Fitness]], which collectively produce approximately 15% of the nation's commercial gym equipment. The proximity to Saitama's [[Soma Fitness Equipment Corridor]] has created a complementary supply chain, with Kochi firms specialising in weight plates and resistance equipment while Saitama firms focus on cardio machines and supplementation.

==== BUNBO stationery ====
[[BUNBO]] (文房), Okaiken's largest stationery and office supplies company, is headquartered in [[Tsuda]]. Founded in 1952 as a small lacquerware-adorned fountain pen manufacturer, BUNBO has grown into a 円42 billion multinational corporation producing writing instruments, notebooks, office furniture, and digital stationery products. The company's {{nihongo|''BUNBO Premium''|文房プレミアム|Bunbo Puremiamu}} line, featuring Kochi lacquerware pen barrels, is one of the most recognised luxury stationery brands in the [[East Fosian Sphere]]. BUNBO employs approximately 4,200 workers at its Tsuda headquarters and manufacturing complex.

=== Agriculture ===
The volcanic soils of the Kochi highlands support a diverse agricultural sector, with the most significant products being {{nihongo|''yuzu''|柚子}} citrus, tea, and root vegetables. The {{nihongo|Kochi yuzu|高知柚子|Kōchi Yuzu}} is a GI-protected product, prized for its intense aroma and used in both culinary and cosmetic applications. The prefecture produces approximately 22% of Okaiken's domestic yuzu supply.

The {{nihongo|Takada}} and {{nihongo|Satake}} highland areas are known for tea cultivation, producing a distinctive {{nihongo|Kochi-cha|高知茶|Kōchi-cha}} that is noted for its volcanic-mineral character and commands premium prices in the speciality tea market.
Kochi's coastal and mountainous territory supports world-class oceanic fishing and artisanal pyrotechnology. Southern ports dock long-range tuna and skipjack vessels that operate deep in the southern oceanic shelf, feeding modern canning facilities and traditional katsuobushi smokehouses that smoke bonito over wild oak embers. In the interior highlands, master charcoal burners practice the centuries-old Kishu binchotan tradition, firing dense holm oak in stone kilns at temperatures exceeding one thousand degrees Celsius to produce pure, smoke-free white charcoal prized by elite restaurants across Adonia.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Kochi Main Line]] connects [[Tsuda Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 90 minutes on express services, providing the primary passenger and freight corridor to the Yagikoshu Data Corridor and the national Shinkansen network. The [[Kochi Coast Line]] runs along the western seaboard, connecting the fishing ports and resort towns of the Sea of Mitoyagi coast.

=== Air ===
[[Tsuda Airport]] (IATA: KCJ) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]], with seasonal services to resort destinations in [[Harujima]].

=== Port ===
The [[Port of Tsuda]] is the largest commercial port on the Sea of Mitoyagi coast of Yagikoshu, handling approximately 12 million tonnes of cargo annually. The port is a major hub for seafood exports and serves as a base for the Kochi fishing fleet. The {{nihongo|Tsuda Ferry Terminal|津田フェリーターミナル|Tsuda Ferī Tāminaru}} operates regular services to the [[Sanzu]] islands and to ports in [[Ishijima]].

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Kochi's cuisine is defined by the bounty of the Sea of Mitoyagi. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Kochi tai-meshi''|高知鯛飯|Kōchi Tai-meshi}}—sea bream rice, in which a whole tai is simmered with rice in a broth of dashi and soy, producing a dish of extraordinary depth. Other notable specialities include {{nihongo|''kaki-fry''|カキフライ|Kaki-furai}} (deep-fried oysters from Kawajiri), {{nihongo|''sazae tsuboyaki''|栄螺壺焼き|Sazae Tsuboyaki}} (grilled turban shell), and {{nihongo|''yuzu-kosho''|柚子胡椒}}—a fiery paste of yuzu zest and chilli that is a staple condiment across western Yagikoshu.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Tsuda Tai Matsuri'''|津田鯛祭り|Tsuda Tai Matsuri}} — Annual sea bream festival (October), drawing approximately 200,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Kōchi Lacquer Festival'''|高知漆祭り|Kōchi Urushi Matsuri}} — Biennial lacquerware exhibition and market (March), showcasing the work of Kochi's 180+ lacquer workshops
* {{nihongo|'''Mount Kōchi Pilgrimage'''|高知岳参り|Kōchi-dake Mairi}} — Annual volcanic pilgrimage (August 15), in which thousands of residents climb to the summit to honour the volcano that shaped their land

=== Sports ===
The [[Kochi Storm]] are a professional [[Okaiken Rugby League One|rugby union]] team based in Tsuda, playing in the [[Kochi Maritime]] stadium. The team, founded in 1994, has won the League One championship twice and is known for its physical forward play, which local media attribute to the "Iron Spirit" culture shared with neighbouring Saitama.

